WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:02.220
- In the world of language nerdery,

00:00:02.220 --> 00:00:04.710
there's perhaps no greater language nerd

00:00:04.710 --> 00:00:07.020
than the conlang language nerd.

00:00:07.020 --> 00:00:09.540
Conlang is short for constructed language,

00:00:09.540 --> 00:00:11.490
and it refers to languages
that don't evolve

00:00:11.490 --> 00:00:14.040
from the organic forces
of culture, but rather,

00:00:14.040 --> 00:00:16.800
were deliberately designed
by one or more individuals.

00:00:16.800 --> 00:00:19.080
Conlangers spend years, decades,

00:00:19.080 --> 00:00:21.540
even lifetimes building their languages.

00:00:21.540 --> 00:00:24.240
And let me tell you,
there is a lot more to it

00:00:24.240 --> 00:00:27.450
than just making up a few
or a few thousand words.

00:00:27.450 --> 00:00:29.280
According to "The
Language Construction Kit"

00:00:29.280 --> 00:00:32.190
by Mark Rosenfelder, you
have to decide on phonetics,

00:00:32.190 --> 00:00:34.500
morphology, orthography, syntax,

00:00:34.500 --> 00:00:36.570
and semantics, just for starters.

00:00:36.570 --> 00:00:37.950
It also helps to be familiar

00:00:37.950 --> 00:00:40.530
with lots of existing languages.

00:00:40.530 --> 00:00:42.630
If English is your only
frame of reference,

00:00:42.630 --> 00:00:44.760
you'll be missing out
on the wonderful variety

00:00:44.760 --> 00:00:47.370
of sounds and systems that
humans have generated.

00:00:47.370 --> 00:00:50.280
Will your language be
agglutinative like Turkish?

00:00:50.280 --> 00:00:53.550
Will it have subject-object-verb
structure like Japanese?

00:00:53.550 --> 00:00:56.940
Or maybe ergative-absolutive
alignment like Basque?

00:00:56.940 --> 00:00:59.220
I told you this was gonna get nerdy.

00:00:59.220 --> 00:01:01.920
It may not seem like there's
much practical application

00:01:01.920 --> 00:01:04.230
for conlangs in the real world,

00:01:04.230 --> 00:01:05.970
but the history of invented languages

00:01:05.970 --> 00:01:08.520
deepens our understanding
of language in general,

00:01:08.520 --> 00:01:11.910
its potential, its
limitations, and its beauty.

00:01:11.910 --> 00:01:14.546
I'm Dr. Erica Brozovsky,
and this is Otherwords.

00:01:14.546 --> 00:01:17.379
(whimsical music)

00:01:21.691 --> 00:01:24.108
♪ Otherwords ♪

00:01:25.410 --> 00:01:29.250
Perhaps the most famous
conlanger is J.R.R. Tolkien,

00:01:29.250 --> 00:01:31.830
linguist and author of
"The Lord of the Rings."

00:01:31.830 --> 00:01:33.870
Creating his fictional elvish languages,

00:01:33.870 --> 00:01:35.280
Quenya and Sindarin,

00:01:35.280 --> 00:01:38.692
was Tolkien's true passion and priority.

00:01:38.692 --> 00:01:41.177
(speaking in Sindarin)

00:01:41.177 --> 00:01:44.592
(speaking in Quenya)

00:01:44.592 --> 00:01:47.258
(speaking in Sindarin)

00:01:47.258 --> 00:01:50.304
(speaking in Quenya)

00:01:50.304 --> 00:01:52.890
He was inspired by the
beauty of Finnish and Welsh,

00:01:52.890 --> 00:01:55.170
and because real languages
evolved with time,

00:01:55.170 --> 00:01:57.450
Tolkien wanted his too as well.

00:01:57.450 --> 00:02:00.180
This required writing a
detailed history and mythology

00:02:00.180 --> 00:02:02.460
of the people who spoke
them, which ended up becoming

00:02:02.460 --> 00:02:05.430
one of the most beloved
fiction franchises of all time,

00:02:05.430 --> 00:02:08.100
and basically, created an entire genre

00:02:08.100 --> 00:02:09.540
of popular entertainment.

00:02:09.540 --> 00:02:11.520
This is more or less the opposite

00:02:11.520 --> 00:02:14.730
of how that other super nerdy
fictional language came about.

00:02:14.730 --> 00:02:18.390
In the early 1980s, a linguist
named Marc Okrand was hired

00:02:18.390 --> 00:02:21.000
by Paramount Pictures to
devise a Klingon language

00:02:21.000 --> 00:02:24.030
for the upcoming third film
in the Star Trek franchise.

00:02:24.030 --> 00:02:27.420
Up to that point, the only
Klingon that had ever been heard

00:02:27.420 --> 00:02:29.940
were a few lines actor
James Doohan came up with

00:02:29.940 --> 00:02:32.504
while filming "Star Trek,
The Motion Picture."

00:02:32.504 --> 00:02:33.870
(speaking in Klingon)

00:02:33.870 --> 00:02:36.990
Okrand figured that since
this was an alien race,

00:02:36.990 --> 00:02:38.160
he ought to choose features

00:02:38.160 --> 00:02:40.350
that are very rare in human languages,

00:02:40.350 --> 00:02:43.710
like an odd object-verb-subject
sentence structure

00:02:43.710 --> 00:02:44.850
and a collection of phonemes

00:02:44.850 --> 00:02:47.100
that are difficult to
pronounce individually,

00:02:47.100 --> 00:02:49.322
let alone in quick succession.

00:02:49.322 --> 00:02:50.998
(speaking in Klingon)
Okay.

00:02:50.998 --> 00:02:54.165
(speaking in Klingon)

00:02:58.967 --> 00:03:00.750
Okrand also wanted the language

00:03:00.750 --> 00:03:02.730
to match the coarse war-like culture

00:03:02.730 --> 00:03:04.380
of the people who speak it.

00:03:04.380 --> 00:03:07.110
So Klingon has many different
synonyms for fighting,

00:03:07.110 --> 00:03:09.210
but no word for hello.

00:03:09.210 --> 00:03:11.850
The closest you can get
is, (speaks in Klingon)

00:03:11.850 --> 00:03:14.100
which means, what do you want?

00:03:14.100 --> 00:03:16.560
Linguist David J. Peterson
took a similar tack

00:03:16.560 --> 00:03:19.590
when inventing Dothraki for
the "Game of Thrones" TV show.

00:03:19.590 --> 00:03:21.900
It has 14 different ways to say horse,

00:03:21.900 --> 00:03:24.000
but none for thank you.

00:03:24.000 --> 00:03:25.290
When helping James Cameron

00:03:25.290 --> 00:03:27.630
develop the Na'vi language for "Avatar,"

00:03:27.630 --> 00:03:29.280
linguist Paul Frommer wanted it

00:03:29.280 --> 00:03:32.190
to be relatively easy for
the actors to pronounce.

00:03:32.190 --> 00:03:34.770
Though he did include
some ejective phonemes,

00:03:34.770 --> 00:03:37.472
voiceless consonants produced
with a contracted glottis,

00:03:37.472 --> 00:03:40.950
like ch'ah and p'ah,
similar to those found

00:03:40.950 --> 00:03:43.800
in real-world languages
like Mayan and Chechen.

00:03:43.800 --> 00:03:47.280
Na'vi does not require specifying
gender in its pronouns,

00:03:47.280 --> 00:03:49.590
though it does offer two forms of we.

00:03:49.590 --> 00:03:53.250
We as in me and you, and we
as in me and someone else.

00:03:53.250 --> 00:03:54.900
That way you always know exactly

00:03:54.900 --> 00:03:56.940
who's invited to the sa'eoio.

00:03:56.940 --> 00:03:58.710
Clearly, it takes a lot of work

00:03:58.710 --> 00:04:01.230
to make a fictional language sound real.

00:04:01.230 --> 00:04:04.590
But does anyone actually
speak them? Not really.

00:04:04.590 --> 00:04:06.030
Of all the fictional languages,

00:04:06.030 --> 00:04:09.600
Klingon has the greatest number
of truly conversant speakers

00:04:09.600 --> 00:04:11.853
and they can probably
all fit onto one bus.

00:04:12.870 --> 00:04:15.270
Fictional languages like Klingon, Quenya,

00:04:15.270 --> 00:04:17.100
and Na'vi were invented mainly

00:04:17.100 --> 00:04:19.110
for artistic and aesthetic purposes.

00:04:19.110 --> 00:04:23.130
However, some language inventors
were much more ambitious.

00:04:23.130 --> 00:04:24.720
They believed their
creations were actually

00:04:24.720 --> 00:04:27.270
improvements over natural languages,

00:04:27.270 --> 00:04:31.080
that they could unlock human
potential and better the world.

00:04:31.080 --> 00:04:34.170
In the 17th century, many
scholars became preoccupied

00:04:34.170 --> 00:04:36.300
with the Tower of Babel problem.

00:04:36.300 --> 00:04:37.440
According to the Bible,

00:04:37.440 --> 00:04:40.260
God cursed humanity with
many different languages

00:04:40.260 --> 00:04:43.260
as punishment for building
a really tall tower.

00:04:43.260 --> 00:04:45.720
The inability to communicate,
these scholars believed,

00:04:45.720 --> 00:04:48.030
was still holding civilization back,

00:04:48.030 --> 00:04:49.350
and they thought they could fix it

00:04:49.350 --> 00:04:51.450
by trying to outdo God once again

00:04:51.450 --> 00:04:53.520
with a man-made universal language.

00:04:53.520 --> 00:04:55.320
The most famous attempt of the time

00:04:55.320 --> 00:04:57.990
was by Bishop John
Wilkins, who didn't like

00:04:57.990 --> 00:05:00.120
that words were just
arbitrary representations

00:05:00.120 --> 00:05:01.950
that had to be memorized.

00:05:01.950 --> 00:05:04.650
He believed that in a
perfect universal language,

00:05:04.650 --> 00:05:06.270
the very essence of a concept

00:05:06.270 --> 00:05:08.520
should be encoded into the word itself,

00:05:08.520 --> 00:05:09.720
with each letter representing

00:05:09.720 --> 00:05:11.850
a hierarchy of classifications.

00:05:11.850 --> 00:05:14.310
For example, a dog was called zita

00:05:14.310 --> 00:05:17.670
because it was a domesticated,
land-dwelling carnivore

00:05:17.670 --> 00:05:19.680
with an oblong head and claws.

00:05:19.680 --> 00:05:22.320
This barely makes sense
when describing animals,

00:05:22.320 --> 00:05:24.480
but Wilkins tried to classify everything,

00:05:24.480 --> 00:05:26.760
and I mean everything!

00:05:26.760 --> 00:05:28.470
The result is hundreds and hundreds

00:05:28.470 --> 00:05:30.780
of pages of complex hierarchies,

00:05:30.780 --> 00:05:32.940
many of which are pretty subjective.

00:05:32.940 --> 00:05:35.220
For example, witchcraft is categorized

00:05:35.220 --> 00:05:38.670
under Judicial Relations,
subheading Capital Crimes.

00:05:38.670 --> 00:05:40.800
As we explained in a previous video,

00:05:40.800 --> 00:05:43.110
what Wilkins didn't
understand, is that words

00:05:43.110 --> 00:05:46.980
being arbitrary memorizations
is not a bug, it's a feature.

00:05:46.980 --> 00:05:49.500
It allows us to share a
vast mental dictionary

00:05:49.500 --> 00:05:52.200
that can be effortlessly
and instantly accessed.

00:05:52.200 --> 00:05:55.050
We don't need to describe
a dog every time we say it

00:05:55.050 --> 00:05:58.350
because we all already
agree on what a dog is.

00:05:58.350 --> 00:06:01.080
Despite the failure of
Wilkin's life's work,

00:06:01.080 --> 00:06:03.600
the dream of a universal
language was resurrected

00:06:03.600 --> 00:06:06.210
in the late 19th century when a small book

00:06:06.210 --> 00:06:08.790
called "Lingvo Internacia"
was self-published

00:06:08.790 --> 00:06:11.340
by someone calling himself Dr. Esperanto,

00:06:11.340 --> 00:06:13.140
which means one who hopes.

00:06:13.140 --> 00:06:16.440
It was a guidebook for an
international auxiliary language,

00:06:16.440 --> 00:06:19.200
a kind of backup language,
which could be used by people

00:06:19.200 --> 00:06:21.930
from different nations to
communicate with each other.

00:06:21.930 --> 00:06:24.630
The true author was L.L. Zamenhof,

00:06:24.630 --> 00:06:26.970
a Jewish ophthalmologist
and amateur linguist

00:06:26.970 --> 00:06:29.550
who grew up in a diverse area of Poland.

00:06:29.550 --> 00:06:31.740
Like Wilkins, Zamenhof believed

00:06:31.740 --> 00:06:34.860
that a universal auxlang
could benefit the human race.

00:06:34.860 --> 00:06:38.310
But unlike Wilkins, he wanted
it to be easy to learn,

00:06:38.310 --> 00:06:39.810
so he borrowed familiar morphemes

00:06:39.810 --> 00:06:41.910
from existing Indo-European languages,

00:06:41.910 --> 00:06:44.940
like hom- for man, and am- for love,

00:06:44.940 --> 00:06:47.910
and a very simple, predictable grammar.

00:06:47.910 --> 00:06:49.230
Anyone with a couple of years

00:06:49.230 --> 00:06:51.300
of Spanish under their
belt should be capable

00:06:51.300 --> 00:06:54.840
of decoding simple sentences,
like kio estas via nomo?

00:06:54.840 --> 00:06:58.770
Today, Esperanto is the biggest
real-world success story

00:06:58.770 --> 00:07:00.990
of all invented languages.

00:07:00.990 --> 00:07:03.180
There are hundreds of
thousands of fluent speakers,

00:07:03.180 --> 00:07:05.640
including several thousand raised on it.

00:07:05.640 --> 00:07:07.680
However, it never quite became

00:07:07.680 --> 00:07:09.750
a universal auxiliary language,

00:07:09.750 --> 00:07:11.700
perhaps due to the rise
in power of Britain

00:07:11.700 --> 00:07:13.950
and the United States after World War II,

00:07:13.950 --> 00:07:17.340
which cemented English's role
as a de facto lingua franca.

00:07:17.340 --> 00:07:20.070
But international diplomacy
isn't the only problem

00:07:20.070 --> 00:07:22.050
that conlangers have tried to fix.

00:07:22.050 --> 00:07:23.460
In the early 20th century,

00:07:23.460 --> 00:07:26.250
a linguist named Benjamin
Whorf hypothesized

00:07:26.250 --> 00:07:27.450
that the language you speak

00:07:27.450 --> 00:07:30.060
limits your perceptions and cognition,

00:07:30.060 --> 00:07:32.010
like maybe having more words for colors

00:07:32.010 --> 00:07:34.890
would actually enable
you to see more colors,

00:07:34.890 --> 00:07:36.210
and maybe Klingons would be nicer

00:07:36.210 --> 00:07:38.220
if they did have a word for hello.

00:07:38.220 --> 00:07:40.590
Some even worried that humanity's reliance

00:07:40.590 --> 00:07:42.390
on language prevented us

00:07:42.390 --> 00:07:45.360
from understanding the true
nature of the universe.

00:07:45.360 --> 00:07:48.573
Words might be Plato's
shadows on the cave wall.

00:07:49.560 --> 00:07:52.200
Sociologist James Cooke Brown wanted

00:07:52.200 --> 00:07:53.940
to test the Whorf hypothesis

00:07:53.940 --> 00:07:56.580
by inventing a language that
could empower its speakers

00:07:56.580 --> 00:07:58.560
to shed all their preconceptions

00:07:58.560 --> 00:08:00.900
and think with perfect logic.

00:08:00.900 --> 00:08:03.390
Loglan, short for logical language,

00:08:03.390 --> 00:08:05.910
is an example of an engineered language,

00:08:05.910 --> 00:08:08.910
one designed for theoretical
or experimental purposes.

00:08:08.910 --> 00:08:11.610
Its grammar was based
on first order logic,

00:08:11.610 --> 00:08:14.280
the kind used in mathematics
and computer science,

00:08:14.280 --> 00:08:16.350
which makes literal translations sound

00:08:16.350 --> 00:08:18.810
more like equations than sentences.

00:08:18.810 --> 00:08:21.990
Loglan was designed to
be totally unambiguous,

00:08:21.990 --> 00:08:25.500
there can only be one possible
way to interpret a sentence.

00:08:25.500 --> 00:08:28.380
You can't just say that Romeo
and Juliet fell in love,

00:08:28.380 --> 00:08:31.230
you must specify whether they
fell in love with each other

00:08:31.230 --> 00:08:33.510
or individually with other people,

00:08:33.510 --> 00:08:36.030
whether they just happen to
be in love while falling,

00:08:36.030 --> 00:08:38.370
or whether they landed in a pile of love.

00:08:38.370 --> 00:08:40.860
All the contextual clues and assumptions

00:08:40.860 --> 00:08:44.130
that lubricate our everyday
conversations are forbidden.

00:08:44.130 --> 00:08:46.350
This makes Loglan so difficult

00:08:46.350 --> 00:08:49.080
that it's unlikely anyone
speaks it fluently enough

00:08:49.080 --> 00:08:51.330
to actually test the Whorf hypothesis.

00:08:51.330 --> 00:08:53.880
Making things even more complicated,

00:08:53.880 --> 00:08:56.580
a disagreement between Brown
and his closest supporters

00:08:56.580 --> 00:08:59.100
created a rift in the Loglan community

00:08:59.100 --> 00:09:02.820
and a competing alternate
version called Lojban.

00:09:02.820 --> 00:09:05.670
Disagreement is a common
theme in conlangs,

00:09:05.670 --> 00:09:08.430
even Esperanto had its
schisms with offshoots

00:09:08.430 --> 00:09:13.050
like Reformed Esperanto, Ido,
Mundolinco, and Romanico.

00:09:13.050 --> 00:09:15.330
No matter how engineered a language is,

00:09:15.330 --> 00:09:17.700
no matter how controlling its creator,

00:09:17.700 --> 00:09:19.350
it will inevitably evolve

00:09:19.350 --> 00:09:21.870
to fit the needs of the people who use it.

00:09:21.870 --> 00:09:23.700
Like the dinosaurs in Jurassic Park,

00:09:23.700 --> 00:09:26.850
language... finds a way.

00:09:26.850 --> 00:09:30.150
Conlangs may not have a stellar
historical success rate,

00:09:30.150 --> 00:09:31.350
but they do a great job

00:09:31.350 --> 00:09:33.810
of revealing how we feel about language.

00:09:33.810 --> 00:09:35.670
We can be entranced by its beauty,

00:09:35.670 --> 00:09:37.290
fascinated by its variety,

00:09:37.290 --> 00:09:39.660
but also frustrated with its limitations,

00:09:39.660 --> 00:09:42.570
and even afraid of its
influence over our thoughts.

00:09:42.570 --> 00:09:43.830
Perhaps there will never be

00:09:43.830 --> 00:09:46.350
a truly universal artificial language,

00:09:46.350 --> 00:09:47.610
but as long as people yearn

00:09:47.610 --> 00:09:49.620
for new ways to express themselves,

00:09:49.620 --> 00:09:52.800
they will always push the
boundaries of what language is.

00:09:52.800 --> 00:09:55.740
We've only scratched the surface
of constructed languages,

00:09:55.740 --> 00:09:58.980
there are over 500 of them
out there by one count.

00:09:58.980 --> 00:10:00.210
If you wanna know more,

00:10:00.210 --> 00:10:01.777
you could check out Arika Okrent's book,

00:10:01.777 --> 00:10:03.897
"In The Land of Invented Languages."

00:10:05.264 --> 00:10:10.264
(speaking in Klingon)
(whimsical music)

00:10:10.452 --> 00:10:15.452
(speaking in Klingon)
(Erica laughs)

00:10:16.257 --> 00:10:19.424
(speaking in Klingon)

